The bug hunt is complete. Let me provide a final summary of my findings.

## Summary

I performed comprehensive end-to-end PRD validation of the stagecoach implementation, building the binary and driving the real CLI against isolated temp git repos with stub providers (single-commit and multi-role decompose) plus real git plumbing. I verified ~45 feature areas across the core commit flow, decompose pipeline, hooks, config, upgrade, locking, and the freeze boundary invariants.

**The implementation is high quality** — `go vet`, `go build`, and `go test -race` are all clean, and the vast majority of the spec (snapshot atomicity, FR-39a identity transparency, FR-M1b/M1d freeze boundary, FR-R5b model-prefix enforcement, token water-fill, hook integration, lock contention, etc.) works correctly.

**Two real bugs found, both in the config layer:**

1. **BUG-001 (major)** — `config init --force` always regenerates role blocks for the auto-*detected* provider (pi), so when a user whose default is a single-backend provider (claude/codex/cursor/agy/qwen-code) refreshes their template, they get an inconsistent `[role.stager] provider = "pi"` that makes decomposition hard-fail with a confusing FR-R5b error (`model "sonnet" on pi must be inference/model`). Single-commit is unaffected; only decompose (the stager role) breaks.

2. **BUG-002 (minor)** — The newer-than-binary config-version notice suggests `config init --force to regenerate`, contradicting FR-B4 (the remedy for a too-new file is to upgrade stagecoach; regenerating would downgrade/destroy the newer schema).

No data-corruption, repo-integrity, security, or concurrency bugs were found.

